+## Subtopics
+
+### Bounded 3D Distance Texture Ray Marching
+*   **What it is:** A method using bounded 3D distance textures for ray marching.
+*   **Difference:** Uses explicit texture lookups for distance bounds rather than neural network outputs.
+*   **When to choose:** When hardware acceleration for texture sampling is preferred over neural inference.
+*   **Link:** [Bounded 3D Distance Texture Ray Marching](1115-bounded-3d-distance-texture-ray-marching.md)
+
+### Sparse Point Set Haptic Rendering via Distance Field
+*   **What it is:** Haptic rendering using distance fields derived from sparse point sets.
+*   **Difference:** Focuses on haptic feedback and point-set based geometry rather than differentiable neural shape representation.
+*   **When to choose:** For haptic simulation tasks with sparse data.
+*   **Link:** [Sparse Point Set Haptic Rendering via Distance Field](17003-sparse-point-set-haptic-rendering-via-distance-field.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per ray evaluation (single MLP forward pass), compared to $O(N)$ for sphere tracing where $N$ is the number of steps [S1]. This accelerates inverse rendering algorithms by approximately 15.5× [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the MLP parameters $\Theta_d$, the latent code $\mathbf{z}$, and the high-dimensional feature grids $\mathbf{f}_{pd}$ [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables differentiable rendering with a single forward pass per pixel; supports generative modeling and single-image reconstruction; handles complex topologies without restrictions on watertightness [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-computation of feature grids; view-dependent artifacts may occur if consistency constraints are not met; limited to shapes inside the unit sphere [S1, S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(K)$ per pixel for the forward pass, where $K$ is the number of mixture components. With $K=2$, this is constant time relative to scene complexity, dominated by the neural network inference cost.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(K)$ per query point for storing weights and depths.
+*   **Strengths:** Enables differentiable rendering of shapes with complex topology and occlusions by smoothing the training signal via probability distributions [S1, S2]. Allows extraction of classical unsigned distance fields and higher-order geometry (normals, curvatures) via network derivatives [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** The output depth $d^*$ is still discontinuous, which can cause artifacts in rendering if not handled carefully during the final selection step [S1]. The method relies on neural network capacity to learn the weight transitions.
+*   **When to use:** When training neural implicit representations for 3D shapes, single-image 3D reconstruction, or generative modeling where differentiability is crucial [S1, S2].
+*   **When not to use:** When non-differentiable, hard-cutoff distance fields are sufficient and neural network overhead is prohibitive.
 
 ## Background
-PDDFs were introduced to address the computational cost of sphere tracing in inverse rendering and the difficulty of training neural networks on discontinuous distance fields [S1, S2]. The concept of Directional Distance Fields (DDFs) was first proposed in "FIRe: Fast Inverse Rendering Using Directional and Signed Distance Functions" [S1]. The probabilistic extension, which differentiably models discontinuities, was detailed in "Probabilistic Directed Distance Fields for Ray-Based Shape Representations" [S2]. These methods are used in single-shape fitting, generative modeling, 3D reconstruction, and path tracing [S2].
+Probabilistic Directed Distance Fields were introduced to address the difficulty of training differentiable neural networks with standard DDFs, which are inherently discontinuous functions of position and view direction [S1, S2]. The method was proposed in the paper "Representing 3D Shapes with Probabilistic Directed Distance Fields" (CVPR 2022) and further detailed in "Probabilistic Directed Distance Fields for Ray-Based Shape Representations" (TPAMI 2025) [S1, S2]. It is applied to single-shape fitting, unpaired 3D-aware generative image modeling, and single-image 3D reconstruction tasks [S1, S2].
 
